    "body": "## Summary\n\nPart of #36112. Consolidates `path_fnamecmp()` and `path_fnamencmp()` into the existing `pathcmp()`, so the three path-comparison helpers share one implementation and ~60 lines of duplicated logic in `src/nvim/path.c` are removed.\n\n- `path_fnamecmp()` / `path_fnamencmp()` now delegate to `pathcmp()`.\n- New `path_fold_char()` helper folds `/` and `\\` as equal and applies `utf_fold()` when `fileignorecase` is set.\n- `pathcmp()` now skips a drive letter matching the current drive on MSWIN (previously only `path_fnamencmp()` did). This fixes `same_directory()` and the runtimepath sort for `C:\\x` vs `\\x` on Windows.\n- `map.c` `equal_path_t()` simplified to use `path_fnamecmp()`.\n\n## Notes / potential impact\n\n- `path_fnamencmp()` on non-Windows changed from raw `strncmp()`/`mb_strnicmp()` to `pathcmp()`, so it now ignores a trailing `/` within the compared prefix. All current callers pass `len = strlen(prefix)`, so their behavior is unchanged, but the contract is broader now.\n- `equal_path_t()` now treats `\"dir\"` and `\"dir/\"` as equal while `hash_path_t()` does **not** strip the trailing slash. If both variants are ever stored in a `StringMap<Path>`, the hash↔equality invariant is broken. Flagged for a follow-up (either keep a strict compare in `equal_path_t()` or strip the trailing slash in `hash_path_t()`). See the comment on #36112.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `pathcmp` behavior fuzz-compared against the previous implementations (equality preserved across 100M+ random cases, including the MSWIN drive-skip path).\n- [ ] CI / platform builds.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] `path_fold_char()` / `pathcmp()` reviewed for the `/` vs `\\` and trailing-slash edge cases.\n- [x] No behavior change for existing `path_fnamencmp()` callers (verified `len` is `strlen(prefix)` in every caller).\n\nrefs #36112\n",
